// platform domain -- Go-backed capabilities over the platform's own rows.

// ---- deploy from the Library (memql#4345) ----
//
// Deploying a hosted site is a read-then-validate-then-write dance MemQL
// cannot express on its own: it resolves a Library artifact to its backing
// file row, fetches that file's bytes from object storage, expands a zip,
// enforces size / count limits over the expanded tree, uploads every file
// under a fresh content-addressed version prefix, and only then flips the
// site row's bundleRef. The Go side is component/sitepublish/site_publish.go;
// the atomic upload-then-flip half is component/edge's Publisher, the same
// one POST /sites/{id}/bundles uses, so a portal deploy and a CI deploy
// produce the same versioned prefixes and can roll back across each other.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THIS DECLARATION and is not meant to be. The
// capability runs under the CALLER's actor and issues owner-scoped named
// queries (siteById, libraryArtifactById, libraryFileById) plus one guarded
// write (updateSiteBundle against v1:platform:site's composite tier), so the
// caller must own BOTH the site -- or be a cluster owner -- and the artifact.
// A cross-user call resolves zero rows and is refused by name.
@sdk
@executor("integration.sitePublish.fromArtifact")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Deploy a Library zip artifact to one of the caller's hosted sites (memql#4345). The caller must own the site (or be a cluster owner) AND own the artifact, which must be a Library file whose MIME type is a zip. The bundle is read from object storage and validated -- index.html at the ROOT for spa and shopify_storefront, plus the same per-file (25 MB), whole-bundle (500 MB) and file-count (20000) limits POST /sites/{id}/bundles enforces -- then written under a new content-addressed version prefix before bundleRef is flipped, so a failed publish leaves the site serving exactly what it was serving. artifactId is stamped on the site row as provenance and the attempt is recorded on the security audit log. Returns {siteId, artifactId, fileId, version, bundleRef, fileCount, totalBytes}. Rollback is unchanged: updateSiteBundle pointed back at an earlier version's bundleRef.")
builtin sitePublishFromArtifact {
  siteId      string!  @description("The v1:platform:site row to publish to.")
  artifactId  string!  @description("The v1:library:artifact index row of the zip to deploy.")
}

// ---- Custom domains (epic memql#4805) ----
//
// Two capabilities and a sweep. The split between them is the same one
// sitePublishFromArtifact already draws: a write MemQL cannot express reaches
// the graph through a builtin, and everything the graph CAN express stays a
// mutation.
//
// AUTHORIZATION IS NOT IN THESE DECLARATIONS. v1:platform:customDomain declares
// @rowAuthz(clusterOwner), so every read and write below runs against that tier
// under the CALLER's actor -- an admin who is not a cluster owner resolves zero
// rows and is refused by name. Design D1 is deliberate about the scope: v1
// automates what was already owner territory rather than widening it, because
// certificate issuance and Ingress objects are cluster-level resources with
// real rate limits.

/// The reachable half of creating a binding: mint the ownership token, prefill the account tie, and
/// write the row. `createCustomDomain` itself is @serverOnly precisely so this is the only way in --
/// a caller who chooses their own verification token proves nothing by publishing it.
@sdk
@executor("integration.customDomain.add")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Bind a client's own domain to one of this cluster's deployables (epic memql#4805). Mints the ownership token the client publishes at TXT _memql-verify.<hostname>, prefills the account tie from the site when one exists, and lands the row `pending_dns`. Three guards refuse before anything is written: a hostname under the cluster's own domain (that is <slug>.<domain> territory and createSite owns it), a collision with a front-door host or an existing site or binding, and a claim past MEMQL_CUSTOM_DOMAIN_MAX_PER_SITE. Nothing is issued until BOTH DNS checks pass -- Let's Encrypt rate limits are real, and a domain that merely points at this cluster must not be claimable by whoever asks first. Returns {domainId, hostname, siteId, token, status, verifyRecordName, pointsToTarget, pointsToKind}.")
builtin customDomainAdd {
  siteId    string!  @description("The v1:platform:site row this domain should serve.")
  hostname  string!  @description("The client's own fully qualified host, e.g. www.acme.com or acme.com.")
}

/// One reconciliation pass over every non-terminal binding. The scheduled automation's whole body.
///
/// It does the DNS half itself and dispatches the Kubernetes half through the capability-script
/// seam, which is what keeps client-go out of the engine's module graph (design D2). Both are one
/// builtin because they are one decision: the check's result IS what selects the next action, and
/// splitting them would put the state machine in two places that could disagree about a row.
@executor("integration.customDomain.reconcile")
@args(profile="object")
@description("Run one custom-domain reconciliation pass (epic memql#4805). For each non-terminal v1:platform:customDomain row: verify the TXT ownership token and the pointing record, promote to `issuing` when both pass, dispatch the idempotent bind capability script for `issuing` rows and the unbind script for `removing` ones, and write back what was seen with a typed failureReason. Retries ride this schedule; there is no manual re-check. Returns {checked, verified, issued, removed, failed}.")
builtin customDomainReconcile { }
